<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taprobane</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taprobane was the ancient Greek and Roman name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, derived from Tambapanni, the name of the first Sinhalese settlement. Greek writers learned of the island after the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, and it was described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> and Ptolemy, whose map greatly exaggerated its size. The name stayed in use in European geography until the early modern period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taprobane.jpg" alt="Taprobane" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ptolemy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy%27s_Map_of_Taprobane.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Greek name is thought to derive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> and Pali Tambapanni, in Sanskrit Tamraparni. It may refer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-coloured shores of the island and is tied to the island's origin story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-vijaya">Prince Vijaya</a> and his followers, who later formed the Sinhalese kingdoms. The edicts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> recording his missionary activity also call the island Tambapanni. Greek writers used the forms Taprobanā and Taprobanē, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers Taprobana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> does not mention the island. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> first reported the name to Europeans around 290 BCE, and the treatise De Mundo, ascribed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> or by others to Chrysippus the Stoic, wrongly made it as large as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. It appears in the Geography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a>, whose map of about 194 BCE shows it south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and later in Ptolemy. Strabo, writing under Augustus, noted that Taprobane sent great amounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, tortoise-shell and other merchandise to the markets of India. Stephanus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> named a metropolis of the island Argyra, while Aelian had heard that it had no cities but 750 villages.</p><p>Many place names on Ptolemy's map match those of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>: Anourogrammoi is the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, the port of Bokana is Gokanna, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a>, and the Rogondanai region is Rohana. The identification held in the maps of Abu-Rehan in 1030 and Edrisi in 1154 and in the writing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> in 1292. Later maps confused it: Niccolò de' Conti took Taprobana for a much smaller island, and although Martin Waldseemuller still placed it at Sri Lanka in 1507, Sebastian Munster's map of 1580 made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The name kept appearing in literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a> opens his epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas">Os Lusíadas</a> with the Portuguese barons who sailed beyond Taprobana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tommaso-campanella">Tommaso Campanella</a> mentions the island in The City of the Sun, written in 1602, and in Cervantes's Don Quixote the hero mistakes a drove of sheep for an army from Taprobana. In the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-luis-borges">Jorge Luis Borges</a> mentioned it in The Lottery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and Arthur C. Clarke set the sky elevator of his novel The Fountains of Paradise (1979) in a fictional version of the island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taprobana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
